Let \(g(x) = 1 + x + [x]\), \(\lambda(x) = x + 2[x]\), and \(r(x) = 2+x\). We can write \(g(x)\) and \(\lambda(x)\) as piecewise functions:
g(x) = 1 + x + [x] = \begin{cases} 1+x; & x \in [0,1) \\ 2+x; & x \in [1,2) \\ 5; & x = 2 \end{cases}
\lambda(x) = x + 2[x] = \begin{cases} x; & x \in [0,1) \\ x+2; & x \in [1,2) \\ 6; & x = 2 \end{cases}
Taking the maximum of \(g(x), \lambda(x), r(x)\) over the domain \([0,2]\), we get \(f(x)\):
f(x) = \begin{cases} 2+x; & x \in [0,2) \\ 6; & x = 2 \end{cases}
